    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §64, ABC L",
    "lawSection": "Alcoholic Beverage Control Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S10551\n\nSPONSOR: STEC\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the alcoholic beverage control law, in relation to an\nexemption for certain property in the village of Lake Placid from the\nprohibition of alcohol sales within a certain distance from a church\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo authorize the state Liquor Authority to issue an on-premises license\nto a business within 200 feet of a church within the village of Lake\nPlacid.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 Permits SLA to grant an on-premises license to the business\nlocated at 2442 Main Street in Lake Placid, the former Great Adirondack\nSteak and Seafood restaurant.\n\nSection 2 Provides effective date\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nFor about 40 years, the owners of Great Adirondack Steak and Seafood\nenjoyed an on-premises liquor license at the restaurant, which is\nlocated next door to St. Eustace Episcopal Church.\n\nHowever, when the property was sold in 2025, the license did not trans-\nfer to the new ownership group. After consultation with SLA, the new\nowners have asked for this legislation so the business can remain fully\noperational.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
